## What the reviewer got wrong, or what the product/checker will not allow (not applied)
- a#14 / b#7 / b#8, space grouping: Hungarian does group thousands with a space, but the checker parses only
  dot-grouping for hu, and ACCEPTED-ROUND skips the request. Dots kept; only the code position was fixed.
- a#17, b#42, b#52: the "spare" digits are not spare against the Korean number bag; Hungarian has no numeral
  lexicon in the checker, so writing them as words FAILs `numbers` (outputs quoted above). 007's counts are the
  exception and were converted.
- a#27: the request to render "National Taiwan University" as "Tajvani Nemzeti Egyetem" contradicts R5 and
  ACCEPTED-ROUND (Latin-script proper names stay). Only the orthography of the suffix was fixed.
- a#48: the suggested Korean gloss (의료법) would put Hangul outside `url`, which the hard rules and the
  `hangul` check forbid. The gloss was dropped instead. "3 évig terjedő" keeps its digit.
- a#64: Q16–Q20 are body lines in the Korean source as well, so this is a source-side defect; the `headings`
  check forbids fixing it on the Hungarian side alone.
- b#26 (ZWSP): the zero-width spaces are not a Hungarian defect — the Korean source has the same count and
  `blocks` pins it.
- b#43: written as "A tagok közül 1–3 igazgató választható." rather than "egy–három"; the digits are in the
  Korean bag and the spelled-out form would fail `numbers`. Word order and phrasing follow the reviewer.
- b#35: the reviewer's rewrite also re-ordered the clause; only the redundant "ezért" was removed, because the
  sentence otherwise parses and the block must keep its content.

